-- title:
--   Lemma 11, second sentence — under (H0) and gating, $\gamma\le\varepsilon_0$
-- statement:
--   Consider a routed run whose routes are the signal routes $\rho_j=\rho(\nu_j)$ of the base views for a signal system with correctness signal $\sigma_1=1[V_0\text{ accepts}]$ and gating condition $w_1>1-\theta$. Assume base soundness (H0): in every round $j\le N$,
--
--   $$
--   \mathbb P[\sigma_1(\nu_j)=1\mid\mathcal F_{j-1}]\le\varepsilon_0\quad\text{almost surely.}
--   $$
--
--   Then $\varepsilon_0$ bounds the A-forgery rate: $\mathbb P[\rho_j=A\mid\mathcal F_{j-1}]\le\varepsilon_0$ almost surely in every round, i.e. $\gamma\le\varepsilon_0$.
--
--   Gating therefore lets the soundness of the base protocol control how much mass a prover can push into the weaker arm.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: every prover interacting with the verifier on a false statement → every probability space $(\Omega,\mu)$, every filtration $(\mathcal F_j)$, every family of post-base-stage σ-algebras $\mathcal G_j$ and every adapted run $(\rho,a)$ (`IsRoutedRun`); reason: the run of any prover is such a run, with $\mathcal F_j$ generated by the first $j$ rounds and $\mathcal G_j$ by $\mathcal F_{j-1}$ and $\nu_j$; the theorem specializes to the paper's
--   - encoding: the hypothesis $x\notin L$ → not modelled; reason: the run is the run on a false statement; the hypotheses (H0), (HA) and the forgery bound are what $x\notin L$ supplies
--   - encoding: rounds $j=1,\dots,N$ conditioned on $\mathcal F_{j-1}$, answers $a_j\in\{0,1\}$ → 0-based rounds $j<N$ conditioned on `F j`, answers in `Bool`; reason: relabelling; same instances
--   - encoding: $\mathbb P[S\ge\tau]$ and conditional probabilities $\mathbb P[E\mid\mathcal F]$ almost surely → `μ.real {ω | τ ≤ creditSum …}` and `condProb` (Mathlib `condExp` of the indicator), with `∀ᵐ`; reason: Lean convention; the events are measurable, so these are the paper's quantities
--   - generalization: $\gamma=\sup\mathbb P[\rho_j=A\mid\mathcal F_{j-1}]$ over provers, rounds and histories → any real $\gamma$ with $\mathbb P[\rho_j=A\mid\mathcal F_{j-1}]\le\gamma$ almost surely for $j<N$ (`ForgeryBound`); reason: the paper's $\gamma$ is such a bound for every prover's run
--   - generalization: base soundness error $\varepsilon_0\in[0,1]$ → $\varepsilon_0$ any real; reason: the conclusion holds for every real; it specializes to the paper's
--   - generalization: efficiently computable signals $\sigma_i:\{0,1\}^*\to[0,1]$ → maps $\sigma_i:W\to[0,1]$ on an arbitrary type $W$ of views; reason: the paper uses only that each $\sigma_i$ maps into $[0,1]$ (p.5); bit-string views are an instance
--   - encoding: signals $\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_k$ with weights $w_1,\dots,w_k$, correctness signal $\sigma_1$ with $\sigma_1(\nu)=1[V_0\text{ accepts in }\nu]$ → signals indexed by `Fin (k+1)`, index `0` the correctness signal, with $\sigma_1=\mathbf 1_{\mathrm{acc}_0}$ for the set $\mathrm{acc}_0$ of accepting views (`IsCorrectnessSignal`); reason: relabelling of the printed convention; $V_0$'s acceptance is the set $\mathrm{acc}_0$
--   - encoding: the base view $\nu_j$ produced in step (R1), routed by $\rho_j=\rho(\nu_j)$ → $\nu_j$ is $\mathcal G_j$-measurable into a measurable space $W$, $\mathrm{acc}_0$ is measurable, and $\rho_j=\rho\circ\nu_j$; reason: $\mathcal G_j$ is the information after the base stage, which contains $\nu_j$; on bit strings every set is measurable
-- source:
--   Dumlu, Signal-Routed Two-Arm Verification, STOC 2027 submission manuscript (held version, sha256 d56b3b7a…), Lemma 11, second sentence, p.7

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SigRoute_IsRoutedRun
import Definitions.Def_SigRoute_ArmSound
import Definitions.Def_SigRoute_SignalSystem

namespace SigRoute

open MeasureTheory

/-- Lemma 11 (Gating), second sentence (Dumlu, Signal-Routed Two-Arm Verification, STOC 2027 submission manuscript (held version, sha256 d56b3b7a…), Lemma 11, p.7): if the correctness signal has weight
`w_1 > 1 − θ`, then under (H0), `γ ≤ ε_0`; that is, in a run whose routes are the signal routes of the
base views, `ε_0` bounds `P[ρ_j = A | F_{j−1}]` almost surely in every round.

(H0) is `P[σ_1(ν_j) = 1 | F_{j−1}] ≤ ε_0` in every round `j < N`, almost surely (p.6).

**Formalization Note** The paper's "every prover" is "every probability space `(Ω, μ)`, filtration `F`, post-base-stage σ-algebras `G` and adapted run `(ρ, a)`" (`IsRoutedRun`); rounds are 0-based, so the paper's `F_{j−1}` is `F j`. The base view `ν j` takes values in a measurable space `W` and is
`G j`-measurable (it is produced by the base stage); `ρ j = route ∘ ν j` (step (R2)). The set `acc₀` of
accepting views is measurable (views are bit strings in the paper, where every set is measurable). -/
theorem lemma_11_forgery {Ω : Type*} {m : MeasurableSpace Ω} (μ : Measure Ω)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] (F : Filtration ℕ m) (G : ℕ → MeasurableSpace Ω)
    (ρ : ℕ → Ω → Arm) (a : ℕ → Ω → Bool) (hrun : IsRoutedRun F G ρ a) (N : ℕ)
    {W : Type*} [MeasurableSpace W] {k : ℕ} (S : SignalSystem W k) (acc₀ : Set W)
    (hacc₀ : MeasurableSet acc₀) (hσ₁ : S.IsCorrectnessSignal acc₀)
    (ν : ℕ → Ω → W) (hν : ∀ j, Measurable[G j] (ν j))
    (hroute : ∀ j, ρ j = fun ω => S.route (ν j ω))
    (hgate : 1 - S.θ < S.w 0) (ε₀ : ℝ)
    (hH0 : ∀ j < N, ∀ᵐ ω ∂μ, condProb μ (F j) {ω | S.sig (ν j ω) 0 = 1} ω ≤ ε₀) :
    ForgeryBound μ F ρ N ε₀ := by sorry

end SigRoute
